# IMPORTANT for whoever edits this file next (confirmed against Cloudflare's
# own docs, fix round 1 of Day 3 Task 7): when a request matches more than one
# rule below, and two matching rules set the SAME header name, Cloudflare does
# NOT let the more specific rule win -- it joins both values with a comma.
# "Add a more specific rule to override this" is the wrong instinct here and
# will corrupt the header instead of replacing it. If two rules must set the
# same header for overlapping paths, make the path patterns mutually
# exclusive instead (see the /writing/*.md vs /writing/*/ split below for a
# worked example).
#
# Day 3 Task 3: Astro's static build writes only a prerendered endpoint's
# Response BODY to disk (astro build discards the headers set in
# src/pages/resume.json.ts / resume.md.ts) -- the Content-Type actually
# served comes from Cloudflare's asset-server mime lookup by file extension
# instead. `.md` already resolves to `text/markdown; charset=utf-8` by
# default, but `.json` resolves to `application/json` with no charset, which
# does not match 02 §1's "JSON Resume verbatim" requirement. This rule makes
# the deployed response match the endpoint's own declared header exactly,
# for both formats, so neither depends on the mime table's current defaults.
/resume.json
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8

/resume.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

# Day 3 Task 7 (02 §3): the `.md` variant of every /writing and /work detail
# page (src/pages/writing/[...slug].md.ts, src/pages/work/[...slug].md.ts),
# same reasoning as /resume.md above -- the endpoint's own Content-Type
# header does not survive the static build, and this rule ships the
# explicit charset rather than trusting the mime table's current default.
# Wildcarded (not one line per slug) so a route added later needs no
# _headers edit: `*` greedily matches the rest of the path, `:splat` below
# reuses exactly what it captured.
/writing/*.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

/work/*.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

# The HTML detail pages advertise their `.md` sibling via X-Markdown-Variant
# (Base.astro's `markdownHref` prop carries the same URL into the <link>
# tags -- this header and those tags must never disagree). Verified over
# real HTTP (task-7-report.md): an extensionless request like /writing/foo
# 307-redirects to /writing/foo/ before Cloudflare serves the asset, so this
# targets the trailing-slash form the page is actually served from -- a rule
# keyed on the extensionless path would only ever land on a 307 response
# nothing reads. The trailing `/` after the splat also keeps this rule from
# ever matching a `*.md` request (which has no trailing slash) or the bare
# `/writing/` index (too short to satisfy both the prefix and the trailing
# slash) -- see the file-header note above for why that exclusivity, not a
# more-specific override, is what keeps this from colliding with the
# Content-Type rules above. tests/pages.test.ts's "index pages carry no
# X-Markdown-Variant header" and the .md-response-has-no-header assertion in
# the test above it are the mutation-tested guards for this.
/writing/*/
  X-Markdown-Variant: /writing/:splat.md

/work/*/
  X-Markdown-Variant: /work/:splat.md

# The résumé's own X-Markdown-Variant (fix round 2). /resume had a `.md` twin,
# a `run_worker_first` entry and Accept-negotiation in src/worker.ts, but
# advertised none of it -- the header was missing here and the <link> tag was
# missing from the page (src/pages/resume.astro now passes `markdownHref`).
# An EXACT path, not a pattern: the trailing-slash form is what /resume is
# actually served from (dist/client/resume/index.html, same 307 behaviour the
# /writing/*/ comment above documents), and an exact path cannot overlap
# /resume.md or /resume.json above -- which is what keeps this from being
# comma-joined onto another rule's header, per the file-header note.
/resume/
  X-Markdown-Variant: /resume.md

# Issue #171 (epic #165, "agent readiness"): the home page's own markdown
# twin, src/pages/index.md.ts, and its X-Markdown-Variant header -- same
# reasoning as /resume.md above and /resume/ immediately above, and the same
# exclusivity trick. Astro's static build discards a prerendered endpoint's
# own Content-Type header, so without the first rule the served type would
# fall back to whatever Cloudflare's default `.md` mime mapping is today.
#
# Two separate rules, not one, and on purpose: `/` is an exact path, so it
# cannot also match `/index.md`, and neither rule can be comma-joined onto
# the other (see the file-header note). `/` does overlap the broad `/*` rule
# below, but that rule sets X-For-AI-Agents and X-MCP-Server, and this one
# sets a header neither of those names, so there is nothing for it to be
# comma-joined onto there either.
/index.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

/
  X-Markdown-Variant: /index.md

# Day 3 Task 9 (research appendix B2.6): the industry split observed live
# across Anthropic, OpenAI, Cloudflare, Vercel and Perplexity is that the
# llms.txt LAYER gets browser-friendly `text/plain` while `.md` PAGES get
# semantically correct `text/markdown` -- this site's own /resume.md and
# /writing|work/*.md rules above are the latter; these two are the former.
# text/plain also means /llms-full.txt (which can grow arbitrarily large as
# content is added) reliably renders in a browser tab rather than prompting
# a download the way text/markdown sometimes does (B2.6's honest caveat).
# Same reason these need an explicit rule at all: Astro's static build
# discards a prerendered endpoint's own Content-Type header (see
# src/pages/llms.txt.ts and llms-full.txt.ts), so without this, the served
# type would fall back to whatever Cloudflare's default `.txt` mime mapping
# happens to be today.
/llms.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

/llms-full.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

# Day 3 Task 11 (02 §3): /rss.xml and /feed.json, same reasoning as every
# other rule in this file -- Astro's static build discards a prerendered
# endpoint's own Content-Type header (see src/pages/rss.xml.ts and
# feed.json.ts), so without these, the served type would fall back to
# Cloudflare's default mime-table entry for `.xml`/`.json`, neither of which
# matches what a feed reader expects. `application/rss+xml` is RSS's
# de facto (if never formally registered) media type; `application/feed+json`
# is JSON Feed 1.1's own registered one (jsonfeed.org/version/1.1's Discovery
# section) -- not the more generic `application/json` /resume.json uses for
# an unrelated format.
/rss.xml
  Content-Type: application/rss+xml; charset=utf-8

/feed.json
  Content-Type: application/feed+json; charset=utf-8

# Day 4 Task 14 (roadmap "/.well-known + discovery"; 03 §5): same reasoning
# as every rule above -- Astro's static build discards a prerendered
# endpoint's own Content-Type header (see src/pages/.well-known/mcp.json.ts),
# so without this the served type would fall back to Cloudflare's default
# mime-table entry for `.json`, which omits the charset (the same gap
# /resume.json's own rule above closes).
/.well-known/mcp.json
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8

# The MCP Server Card (SEP-1649), same reasoning as the rule above it: Astro's
# static build discards a prerendered endpoint's own Content-Type, so without
# this the served type falls back to Cloudflare's mime-table entry for `.json`,
# which omits the charset.
/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8

# Issue #167 (epic #165, "agent readiness"): RFC 9728 protected-resource
# metadata (src/pages/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource.ts). This path is
# EXTENSIONLESS, unlike every `.json`/`.md` rule elsewhere in this file, so
# Cloudflare's mime lookup has nothing to guess a type from at all -- this
# rule is the only thing standing between the document and whatever fallback
# content type an extensionless static asset gets, which is not a usable one.
/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8

# The prose half of the same surface, auth.md (src/pages/auth.md.ts).
# Cloudflare's asset server already maps `.md` to `text/markdown; charset=utf-8`
# by default -- /resume.md.ts relies on exactly that default with no rule of
# its own -- but this rule is added anyway so the two halves of this surface
# ship their Content-Type the same explicit way rather than one depending on
# a default and the other not.
/auth.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

# Issue #168 (epic #165, "agent readiness"): the API catalog (RFC 9727) and
# the ARD manifest, both built from src/lib/discovery/surface.ts's one list.
#
# /.well-known/api-catalog is EXTENSIONLESS, same situation as
# /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource above -- Cloudflare's mime lookup has
# nothing to guess a type from at all, so this rule is the only thing
# standing between the document and whatever fallback content type an
# extensionless static asset gets. application/linkset+json is RFC 9264's
# registered type for a linkset document and RFC 9727 requires it for an API
# catalog; no charset, because RFC 9264 registers the type without one.
/.well-known/api-catalog
  Content-Type: application/linkset+json

# /.well-known/ai-catalog.json additionally carries Access-Control-Allow-
# Origin, required by the ARD specification so a browser-resident agent on
# another origin can read the manifest with `fetch()` rather than failing the
# browser's own CORS check. No other rule in this file sets either header for
# this exact path, so there is nothing here for Cloudflare to comma-join --
# see the file-header note if that stops being true.
/.well-known/ai-catalog.json
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

# Issue #169 (epic #165, "agent readiness"): the agent skills discovery index
# (src/lib/discovery/skills.ts, served by src/pages/.well-known/agent-skills/
# index.json.ts) and the SKILL.md behind each entry
# (src/pages/.well-known/agent-skills/[skill]/SKILL.md.ts).
#
# /.well-known/agent-skills/index.json is EXTENSIONLESS-STYLE in the sense
# that matters here even though it ends in `.json`: same reasoning as every
# other rule in this file -- Astro's static build discards a prerendered
# endpoint's own Content-Type, so without this the served type falls back to
# Cloudflare's mime-table entry for `.json`, which omits the charset.
#
# The wildcard rule below and this exact one cannot both match one path
# (`/.well-known/agent-skills/index.json` is not beneath
# `/.well-known/agent-skills/*/SKILL.md`, and the second pattern's literal
# `SKILL.md` segment cannot match a path ending `index.json`), so there is
# nothing here for Cloudflare to comma-join -- see the file-header note for
# why that would matter if it were otherwise. tests/discovery-skills.test.ts's
# served-response assertions are the check that would catch a rewrite of
# either pattern into one that overlapped the other.
/.well-known/agent-skills/index.json
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8

/.well-known/agent-skills/*/SKILL.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

# Day 4 Task 14 (08 v1 item 2): the two discovery headers, verbatim, on
# every response this origin serves FROM A STATIC ASSET. `/*` is the broadest
# pattern in this file and sets no header any rule above also sets, so there is
# nothing for it to be comma-joined with -- see the file-header note for why
# that would matter if there were.
#
# "EVERY RESPONSE" IS NOT LITERALLY TRUE, and the exception is /mcp. This was
# the open question deferred from Day 4 Task 14 and closed by issue #28; the
# answer is no, and the reason is not the one it looks like. MEASURED against
# production 2026-09-07:
#
#   curl -sI https://ryanlindsey.me/                    -> X-MCP-Server present
#   curl -sI https://ryanlindsey.me/llms.txt            -> present
#   curl -sI https://ryanlindsey.me/resume              -> present
#   curl -sI https://ryanlindsey.me/work/silent-failure/ -> present
#   curl -sI https://ryanlindsey.me/mcp                 -> ABSENT (405)
#   POST   https://ryanlindsey.me/mcp                   -> ABSENT (200)
#
# The obvious explanation -- "/mcp is routed by `run_worker_first`, so the
# asset server never sees it" -- IS WRONG, and the third and fourth lines above
# are the control that disproves it: /resume and /work/* are in that same
# `run_worker_first` list (./wrangler.jsonc) and carry the headers fine. What
# actually decides it is whether the response BODY came from a static asset.
# These rules are applied by Cloudflare's asset server, so they attach to any
# asset response -- including one src/worker.ts fetched itself through the
# `ASSETS` binding after running first. /mcp has no asset behind it at all: the
# response is synthesised by the MCP Worker and forwarded back, and there is
# nothing for the asset server to decorate.
#
# Left as-is deliberately. The header exists so a crawler landing on a PAGE
# learns the endpoint exists, and every page it can land on is an asset and does
# carry it; an MCP client that has already reached /mcp is speaking the protocol
# and has no use for a header advertising /mcp. Injecting these in the Worker
# would mean src/worker.ts growing header logic that duplicates this file, which
# is the thing that file-header note exists to warn about. Recorded here so it is
# not rediscovered a third time.
#
# Issue #170 (epic #165, "agent readiness"): `Link` joins the two headers above
# on this SAME `/*` rule rather than arriving as a second `/*` rule of its own.
# This is ONE RULE setting ONE HEADER whose value happens to be comma-separated
# -- that comma-separated list is RFC 8288's own `Link` field-value syntax, not
# two _headers rules being joined the way the file-header note above warns
# about. A second `/*` rule that also set `Link` would still parse and still
# mean something, which is the worst kind of wrong: do not "fix" this into one.
# The four targets are the discovery documents issues #166-#169 shipped
# (the MCP Server Card, the RFC 9727 API catalog, the agent skills index, and
# /llms.txt); `rel` values are the ones IANA's Link Relations registry defines
# for each (service-desc, api-catalog, describedby, service-doc).
# tests/discovery-link-headers.test.ts pins the parsed relations, fetches every
# target for a live 200, and counts the `</` occurrences to catch a reversion to
# two joined rules.
/*
  X-For-AI-Agents: You're welcome here. Start at /llms.txt
  X-MCP-Server: https://ryanlindsey.me/mcp
  Link: </.well-known/mcp/server-card.json>; rel="service-desc", </.well-known/api-catalog>; rel="api-catalog", </.well-known/agent-skills/index.json>; rel="describedby", </llms.txt>; rel="service-doc"
